<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gdynia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gdynia is a port city on the Baltic coast of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>. A fishing village when Poland regained access to the sea in 1920, it was developed by the Second Polish Republic from the 1920s into a modern port independent of the Free City of Danzig. By the late 1930s it was one of the busiest ports on the Baltic, and with Gdańsk and Sopot it now forms the Tricity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gdynia.jpg" alt="Gdynia" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kontrola · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:00_sea_towers_(April_2018).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
